.TH "vsearch-usearch" "7" "September 18, 2026" "version 2.32.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
usearch compatibility \[em] the commands, options and behaviours vsearch
shares with usearch, and those it does not
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
If you are a usearch user, our objective is to make you feel at home:
vsearch was designed to behave like usearch, to some extent.
Like any complex software, usearch is not free from quirks and
inconsistencies.
We decided not to reproduce some of them, and, for complete
transparency, this page documents the differences that matter when a
usearch command line is ported to vsearch: commands vsearch does not
implement, options it spells differently, options it accepts and
ignores, options it rejects, and behaviours it changed on purpose.
.PP
Two differences are broad enough to state first.
vsearch works with nucleotide sequences only; amino acid sequences are
not supported (see \f[V]vsearch-nucleotides(7)\f[R]).
And vsearch always aligns globally, with full dynamic programming, so
the options by which usearch tunes or bounds its alignment stage have
nothing to act on (see
\f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R]).
.SH COMMANDS NOT IMPLEMENTED
.TP
\f[V]search_global\f[R]
usearch\[cq]s exhaustive database search, as opposed to the heuristic
\f[V]usearch_global\f[R].
vsearch has no separate command for it because the heuristics can be
switched off:
\f[V]--usearch_global --maxaccepts 0 --maxrejects 0 --minwordmatches 0\f[R]
compares every query to every target.
The first two remove the early stop, the third removes the word
pre-filter that selects candidates, and the alignment itself is always a
full dynamic programming alignment.
Expect the runtime that implies.
.TP
\f[V]cluster_otus\f[R]
Not implemented, and there is no exact equivalent.
It combines greedy clustering with chimera filtering (the UPARSE-OTU
algorithm), so the closest vsearch route is a clustering command
followed by a chimera-detection command, run separately.
.TP
\f[V]search_pcr\f[R], \f[V]search_oligodb\f[R]
Not implemented.
Extracting the region between two primers is not something vsearch does;
\f[V]--cut\f[R] cuts at a restriction pattern, which is a different
operation (see \f[V]vsearch-cut(1)\f[R]).
.TP
\f[V]otutab_rare\f[R], \f[V]otutab_norm\f[R]
Not implemented.
Rarefying or normalising an OTU table to a common number of reads is
left to downstream tools.
\f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R] subsamples \f[I]sequences\f[R], not table
columns (see \f[V]vsearch-fastx_subsample(1)\f[R]).
.TP
\f[V]otutab_octave\f[R]
Not implemented.
.TP
\f[V]sintax_summary\f[R]
Not implemented.
\f[V]--sintax\f[R] writes per-query classifications; summing them per
rank is left to downstream tools (see \f[V]vsearch-sintax(1)\f[R]).
.SH OPTIONS SPELLED DIFFERENTLY
.PP
Same quantity, different name, and in one case a different direction.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
usearch
T}@T{
vsearch
T}
_
T{
\f[V]--fastq_pctid\f[R] \f[I]x\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R] 100 - \f[I]x\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]--nowordcountreject\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]--minwordmatches 0\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]--queryalnfract\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]--query_cov\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]--targetalnfract\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]--target_cov\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
\f[V]--fastq_pctid\f[R] bounds the identity of the overlap from below,
where \f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R] bounds the percentage of mismatches
from above, so the two arguments are complements.
Older usearch versions had \f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R] itself, under
that name.
.SH OPTIONS ACCEPTED AND IGNORED
.PP
These options are recognised so that an existing command line still
runs, but they have no effect.
\f[V]--band\f[R], \f[V]--fulldp\f[R], \f[V]--hspw\f[R],
\f[V]--minhsp\f[R], \f[V]--pattern\f[R], \f[V]--slots\f[R] and
\f[V]--xdrop_nw\f[R] all describe usearch\[cq]s alignment or seeding
stage, which vsearch does not share: vsearch always performs a full
dynamic programming alignment, so there is no band to widen and no
heuristic to relax.
.PP
\f[V]--cons_truncate\f[R] is also accepted and ignored, with a warning.
.PP
\f[V]--strand plus\f[R] is accepted by \f[V]--uchime_ref\f[R] for
compatibility, and is the only value it takes; \f[V]--strand both\f[R]
is a fatal error there.
.SH OPTIONS NOT RECOGNISED
.PP
Some options of usearch 5, 6 and 7, used by pipelines of that era such
as QIIME 1, are rejected outright rather than ignored:
.TP
\f[V]--global\f[R]
vsearch always aligns globally, so the option would assert the only
behaviour there is.
Remove it.
.TP
\f[V]--evalue\f[R]
vsearch does not compute E-values for nucleotide alignments.
The \f[V]evalue\f[R] field of \f[V]--userfields\f[R] exists, and always
reports -1 (see \f[V]vsearch-userfields(7)\f[R]).
.TP
\f[V]--query\f[R]
Not an option in vsearch.
The query file is the argument to the search command itself, as in
\f[V]--usearch_global queries.fasta\f[R].
The name survives as the \f[V]query\f[R] field of
\f[V]--userfields\f[R].
.SH DELIBERATE DIFFERENCES
.PP
Corrections and extensions vsearch applies on purpose.
They are differences in output or in accepted input, not accidents.
.IP \[bu] 2
With \f[V]--blast6out\f[R] and \f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R], usearch
reports 13 fields for a query with no match, where the format has 12.
vsearch reports the 12 the format calls for.
.IP \[bu] 2
With \f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R], usearch lists queries without a match
in the \f[V]--blast6out\f[R] file but not in the alignment output.
vsearch lists them in both, as \f[V]No hits\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]raw\f[R] field of \f[V]--userfields\f[R] is not informative in
usearch.
vsearch reports the alignment score.
.IP \[bu] 2
The fields \f[V]qlo\f[R], \f[V]qhi\f[R], \f[V]tlo\f[R] and \f[V]thi\f[R]
have counterparts \f[V]qilo\f[R], \f[V]qihi\f[R], \f[V]tilo\f[R] and
\f[V]tihi\f[R] reporting alignment coordinates that ignore terminal
gaps.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--iddef\f[R], and with it the alternative pairwise identity
definitions that usearch removed, is available in vsearch.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--topn\f[R] extends to the sorting commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--sizein\f[R] extends to dereplication and clustering.
.IP \[bu] 2
T and U count as the same nucleotide during dereplication, so an RNA and
a DNA spelling of one sequence collapse into a single entry.
.IP \[bu] 2
Sorting is stabilised, using sequence abundances or labels as secondary
and tertiary keys, so equal keys do not come out in an arbitrary order.
.IP \[bu] 2
Low-complexity regions are masked with the DUST algorithm by default,
and masking behaviour is more consistent (see
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_mask(1)\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
vsearch adds the command \f[V]--cluster_size\f[R], which sorts sequences
by decreasing abundance before clustering.
.PP
vsearch also provides commands and options that usearch does not; they
are listed in \f[V]vsearch(1)\f[R] and described one page per command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\f[V]vsearch(1)\f[R], \f[V]vsearch-nucleotides(7)\f[R],
\f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R],
\f[V]vsearch-userfields(7)\f[R], \f[V]vsearch-usearch_global(1)\f[R]
